{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"0a8c: held-out validation initially 16/16 GREEN and 3/3 mutations killed, BUT the read-only dry-run against the real store exposed a REAL BUG my oracle missed. rewrite_description is ORDER-SENSITIVE: when '## Success Criteria' precedes '## Acceptance Criteria' in the document (which ~half the real tickets do), the merge keeps the SC heading, duplicates the criterion, duplicates the whole AC heading+block, and is not idempotent. My oracle only exercised AC-then-SC ordering - a genuine gap on my side, not the implementer's. Running the live migration would have corrupted a large share of 104 shared tickets. This vindicates dry-running before any live store mutation. Added 4 RED cases (SC-before-AC merge, no-duplicate-heading x3 orders, heading-on-own-line, idempotency for SC-first) and re-dispatched the SAME tier per the escalation rule (a first held-out edge failure is the oracle working as intended, not model weakness). Dry-run sizing: 104 tickets total = 91 closed + 10 idea + 2 open + 1 in_progress; NONE of the 3 live ones is actively claimed (no claimed_session, no assignee). Change 971 pushed for the script."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1784957145295065001,"uuid":"ebc6c5d6-fac8-4597-acac-f77ee54753c8"}